## Data comes from outside

A component does not invent every word it shows. Props are values that flow in from the caller. `Badge` should return `props.label`. The call already passes `{ label: "new" }`. The child reads what it was given. It does not look up a global, and it does not ignore the argument and invent a prettier word.

This bites when `Badge` returns a hardcoded `"x"` or `"new"`. Hardcoding `"new"` passes the first screenshot and then fails the moment the parent passes a different label. The word on screen must come from `props`, not from a string you typed inside the child and hoped would stay true.

```javascript
function Badge(props) {
  return props.label;
}
console.log(Badge({ label: "new" }));
```

A common mistake is returning a fixed string, or reading `label` as a bare variable instead of `props.label`. The exercise asks you to return the label from props so the print is `new`, and the source must mention `props`.
